# croft.loom — a stone longhouse on a grass shelf, late afternoon.
#
#     loom render assets/test/croft.loom --out croft.png --size 1280x800 --sim 600
#
# **The subject is the building.** Everything else — the shelf it stands on, the
# bank behind it, the grass, the props — is here to make one house legible, and
# each of them is sized against the house rather than against the world.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three volumes, and why a building is not one
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A `VoxelVolume` carries **one** `Material` plus **one** slope-keyed `layer`,
# and `groundLayerWeight` is `1 - saturate((n.y - cutoff) / fade)` — a rule
# about which way a face points, not about which part of the building it is. So
# a single volume can hold exactly two substances, split flat-versus-steep. A
# house wants three: the roof, the walls, and the dressed stone that caps every
# horizontal (plinth ledge, sills, lintels, chimney cap, threshold).
#
# Walls and caps are the *natural* pair for one volume, because they really are
# split steep-versus-flat. The roof is not: it is flat-ish like a cap and made
# of something else entirely. Hence:
#
#   `Ground` — turf on the flats, bedrock on the steeps, 0.42 m voxels
#   `House`  — dressed stone on the flats, rubble masonry on the walls, 0.16 m
#   `Roof`   — slate, one material, no layer
#
# **The roof has no `layer` and must not get one**, because both its pitches
# are the same slope: a slope rule cannot tell the two apart, and any cutoff
# either paints the whole roof or none of it. The material break at the eaves
# is a *volume* break.
#
# **Each volume's op coordinates are that volume's own space**, and the three
# offsets are the node transforms below. They are written into the section
# headers, because getting one wrong is the whole class of bug in this file:
#
#   Ground  local = world - [ 4.0, 0.0,  4.0]
#   House   local = world - [19.0, 7.0, 14.0]
#   Roof    local = world - [18.0, 11.0, 14.0]
#
# In WORLD terms the house centre is [24, ., 24], the shelf it stands on is at
# y = 9.75, its floor is 10.0, its eaves 12.70 and its ridge 15.05.
#
# **`scene_volume` takes the FIRST volume with a `VoxelVolume` and only that
# one**, and grass, rain shelter and the flow field all read it. So `Ground` is
# first on purpose: grass must march the terrain, not the roof. The corollary
# is that `loom_grass` has no idea the house exists — the four fields below are
# each placed clear of its footprint by hand, because nothing else will.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The light, which is half of it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The camera stands 2.2 m over the shelf, 15.2 m out, on a bearing that puts
# **both** visible faces at about 45 degrees. That is the whole reason for the
# 63-degree stand-off: a three-quarter view where one face is at 10 degrees and
# the other at 80 is not a three-quarter view, it is an elevation with a sliver,
# and the first camera here was exactly that — 77 degrees on the gable.
#
# Its yaw is then 2 degrees off the bearing, at 29 rather than 27. Aiming the
# lens at the house's *centre* is not the same as centring the house: the near
# gable reaches further to one side than the far end does to the other, so on
# the exact bearing the mass sits left and the frame is right-heavy.
#
# The sun is 17.5 degrees up and swung round so it rakes the long façade at
# about 60 degrees of incidence and leaves the gable end — the face with the
# door in it — **entirely unlit**. Both halves of that matter:
#
# - Raking, so the eaves overhang lays a shadow band down the lit wall and the
#   sills and lintels each throw one. Flat noon light hides every bit of that.
# - The gable dark, because the door opens on it and there is a fire inside.
#   A raymarched flame is additive and its dim outer regions read as detached
#   shards against anything bright (ADR 0020); against an unlit stone wall it
#   reads as fire. The composition and the shader want the same thing here.
#
# Ray-traced soft shadows and AO (ADR 0019) are what make this work at all, and
# they see voxel meshes: the eaves shadow, the contact darkening where the bank
# piles against the back wall, and the black inside the window reveals are all
# traced rather than painted.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the building is made of, and the rules each detail is paying
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Nothing thinner than two voxels survives.** At 0.16 m that is 0.32 m, and
# it sets every dimension in the House volume: walls 0.50, sills and lintels
# 0.32 thick and 0.22 proud, the chimney cap 0.40, the eaves fascia 0.35. A
# 0.15 m sill would have vanished silently, which is the failure this rule
# exists for.
#
# **`round` is what stops it being a CAD box**, and it needs to be more than one
# voxel to exist at all: 0.22 m on the walls is 1.4 voxels and reads as the worn
# arris of a rubble wall, 0.30 m on the plinth as a weathered footing. A 0.10 m
# fillet at this resolution is *nothing* — sub-voxel, and the mesher cannot
# place a vertex for it.
#
# **`yaw_degrees` is doing two different jobs.** The whole house is 18 degrees
# off the world axes so that neither its walls nor its roof line up with
# anything else in the scene; and the byre at the far end is at +8, which is 26
# degrees off the house, so the building reads as something added to over time
# rather than extruded from a grid. Every op in an assembly carries the same
# yaw AND has its centre rotated about the assembly's own pivot — yaw turns an
# op about *itself*, so yawing six walls in place spins each on the spot and
# leaves the box open at its corners. `lanternhead.loom` records that trap; the
# numbers here were generated from offsets rather than typed.
#
# **The roof is a solid mass with two planes cut off it**, which is the only
# way to get a pitched roof out of ops that cannot tilt. A `box` has yaw and
# nothing else, and a `capsule` is round in section — so the roof is a box and
# two `subtract` spheres of radius 300, tangent to the pitch at its midpoint.
# Over the 4.25 m the pitch actually runs, the sphere departs from a true plane
# by under a centimetre, and by 5 cm at the far corners of the roof — a roof
# that bellies up 5 cm at its ends, which is the wrong way for an old roof to
# sag but is under half a voxel either way. Three ops for a clean gable.
#
# **The order of the two roofs is load-bearing and is not a style choice.** A
# `subtract` applies to everything accumulated so far, and a roof plane extended
# is an infinite plane — so the byre's cutting spheres would slice the main roof
# if they ran after it. They run first, when only the byre's own mass exists.
# The main roof's spheres then run with both present, which is safe only because
# the byre sits entirely *under* the main roof's planes extended — checked, and
# the tightest clearance is 0.57 m at the byre's front eaves. Move the byre
# outward or raise its ridge and that stops being true, silently, by shaving the
# top off it.
#
# **The building sits in its ground rather than on it** three ways: the bank
# behind it piles 0.67 m up the back wall at turf level and nothing 0.5 m up,
# the plinth stands 0.25 m proud of the turf with a ledge the walls step in
# from, and both boulders lie in dishes the terrain carves — `moraine.loom`'s rule, that a mesh meeting
# voxel ground under a low sun shows its seam unless the ground hollows for it.
# A `subtract` sphere makes a dish only if its centre is ABOVE the ground:
# `centre_y = ground - depth + radius`, or it excavates a crater.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scale, which a building has no way to assert on its own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A stone box is any size at all until something in the frame is not. The door
# is 1.10 x 2.10 m, the windows 1.10 x 1.20, the wall lantern beside the door
# is the imported 0.48 m model at 1.6x, the chopping block is the 0.55 m stump
# and the felled log is 3.0 m. Those are the real metric sizes of the imported
# meshes, and each one carries **its own atlas** — using one prop's atlas on
# another smears it, which is a bug already in this project's history.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cost, measured, and where it is
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `loom run assets/test/croft.loom --edit --play --frames 90` on the **debug**
# build, which is the one `cargo xtask validate` gates on:
#
#     the whole scene            26.05 ms/frame   (worst frame 2265 ms)
#     with `Roof` deleted        22.58            -> the roof volume  3.5 ms
#     with `House` deleted       12.88            -> the house volume 13.2 ms
#     with the four fields gone  23.29            -> all the grass     2.8 ms
#                                                 -> ground + frame    6.6 ms
#
# **Almost all of it is the one-off bake amortised over 90 frames** — the worst
# frame is 2265 ms, which is 25.2 of the 26.0, so the steady state is under a
# millisecond. And it is the House volume, which is 18 ops over 16 chunks at
# 0.16 m: bake cost is per chunk times ops, and this is the file that spends
# both. The terrain is 18 chunks at 0.42 m precisely so that it can.
#
# **This scene is in `SCENES` but deliberately not in the `--play` list**, whose
# ceiling is 30 ms: 26.0 leaves 13% and that is variance rather than headroom.
# Coarsening the House to 0.20 m would buy about a quarter of it and cost the
# sills, which are 0.32 m and need two voxels; the measurement is what says the
# trade is not worth taking.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately not here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No rain and no water. Rain would make this a weather scene and every rain
# scene in the library already is one; water needs a `WaterBody`, which has no
# extent, so a pond on a hilltop is an infinite plane at the pond's height.
#
# No `Scatter` and no trees. `Scatter` gives a whole field one flat albedo with
# `material: u32::MAX`, and the tree cards are alpha cutouts that `fragmentMain`
# has no `discard` for — both would put an unlit rectangle next to the thing the
# scene exists to show.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "e425b440-491c-4495-a5b3-4a9ebf7756f2"

